title: Choosing one of four
mode: draw
chapter: Choosing
check: behaves
parts: chip:my_mux
needs: my_mux
---- intro
Your **my_mux** chooses between two inputs. To choose between **four**, `d0` to `d3`, you need a two-bit number `s1 s0` to say which one, and three of your multiplexers, in two rounds, like a knockout tournament:

- round one: `s0` picks between `d0` and `d1`, and also between `d2` and `d3`
- round two: `s1` picks between the two winners

| s1 | s0 | y |
|----|----|---|
| 0 | 0 | `d0` |
| 0 | 1 | `d1` |
| 1 | 0 | `d2` |
| 1 | 1 | `d3` |
---- task
Make `y` follow the input that `s1 s0` points at, using three **my_mux** chips.
---- start
input d0 0 0
input d1 0 60
input d2 0 140
input d3 0 200
input s0 0 280
input s1 0 340
led y 480 100
---- reference
input d0, d1, d2, d3, s0, s1
led y
lo = (d0 & ~s0) | (d1 & s0)
hi = (d2 & ~s0) | (d3 & s0)
y = (lo & ~s1) | (hi & s1)
---- answer
input d0 0 0
input d1 0 60
input d2 0 140
input d3 0 200
input s0 0 280
input s1 0 340
chip m1 my_mux 160 10
chip m2 my_mux 160 150
chip m3 my_mux 320 90
led y 480 100
d0 -> m1.a
d1 -> m1.b
s0 -> m1.s
d2 -> m2.a
d3 -> m2.b
s0 -> m2.s
m1.y -> m3.a
m2.y -> m3.b
s1 -> m3.s
m3.y -> y
---- hint1
The first multiplexer gets `d0` and `d1`, the second `d2` and `d3`. Both are chosen by `s0`.
---- hint2
The third multiplexer gets the first one's `y` as `a` and the second one's as `b`, with `s1` choosing. Its `y` goes to the light.
---- solution
Two multiplexers on `s0` pick `d0` or `d1`, and `d2` or `d3`. A third on `s1` picks which of those two answers goes to `y`. With `s1 s0` = 1 0, the second mux passes `d2` and the last one passes it on.
---- done
Choosing one of eight would be one more round; one of 32 is how a processor picks one register to read. Next: circuits that remember.
